[size=5][b]FUSE[/b][/size]

FUSE is a modding layer for Railroader. It loads FUSE packages — custom maps, routes, scenery, industries, and audio — and runs your existing legacy mods through a built-in compatibility layer.

[line]

[size=4][b]What it adds[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Custom maps[/b] — whole replacement worlds, picked from a map dropdown on the New Game screen
[*][b]Routes and track[/b] — extensions, new segments, switches, turntables
[*][b]World scenery[/b] — buildings, map labels, speed signs, telegraph poles
[*][b]Operations[/b] — industries, loads, stations, passenger stops, team tracks, interchanges
[*][b]Progression[/b] — sections, delivery phases, and unlock-gated map features
[*][b]Audio[/b] — whistles, horns, and bells
[/list]

FUSE itself adds no content. It is the layer that loads content packages.

[line]

[size=4][b]Your existing mods keep working[/b][/size]

Packages built for [b]Railloader[/b], [b]Strange Customs[/b], [b]ConfusingSupplements[/b], [b]For Your Convenience[/b], and [b]Alina's Map Mod[/b] load through FUSE's compatibility layer. Package authors can also convert them to the FUSE format with the converter tool on GitHub.

[line]

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Railroader [b]2025.1.x[/b]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/site/mods/21]Unity Mod Manager[/url]
[/list]

[line]

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Install Unity Mod Manager and point it at your Railroader install.
[*]Download the FUSE zip from the [b]Files[/b] tab.
[*]Drag the zip onto Unity Mod Manager's [b]Mods[/b] tab. To do it by hand instead, extract the zip so the [b]FUSE[/b] folder inside it lands at [i]Railroader/Mods/FUSE[/i].
[*]Start Railroader and load a map.
[/list]

Open the console and run [b]/fuse.report[/b] to confirm it loaded. The FUSE icon also appears in the top bar.

[b]Do not nest the folder.[/b] [i]Railroader/Mods/FUSE/FUSE/FUSE.dll[/i] is wrong and FUSE will not load.

[line]

[size=4][b]Installing content packages[/b][/size]

Each FUSE package is its own folder under [i]Railroader/Mods[/i], installed exactly like FUSE itself. Install any asset packs a package lists as required, then run [b]/fuse.loaded[/b] to confirm FUSE picked them up.

FUSE respects Unity Mod Manager's enabled checkbox — a package you disable in UMM is not loaded.

[line]

[size=4][b]Updating[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Back up your saves and your current [i]Railroader/Mods/FUSE[/i] folder.
[*]Replace [i]Railroader/Mods/FUSE[/i] with the new version.
[*]Start the game and run [b]/fuse.report[/b].
[/list]

Do not leave a second copy of FUSE anywhere under [i]Railroader/Mods[/i].

[line]

[size=4][b]Uninstalling[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Exit Railroader.
[*]Remove or disable your FUSE packages first.
[*]Remove or disable [i]Railroader/Mods/FUSE[/i].
[*]Start the game and confirm it loads normally.
[/list]

Removing FUSE while packages that depend on it are still installed will leave those packages inert.

[line]

[size=4][b]Good to know[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Multiplayer is compatibility mode only.[/b] FUSE does not sync package contents over the network. Every host and client applies its own local package stack, so everyone needs the same packages installed — the same expectation legacy Railloader had.
[*]FUSE does not add rolling stock or locomotive mods, apart from audio definitions it can import.
[*]FUSE loads data, asset, and audio packages. Legacy script mods that are not data packages are not supported.
[*]The in-game editor was retired in 1.0. Authoring happens in the standalone external editor.
[/list]

[line]

[size=4][b]Having trouble?[/b][/size]

Post a bug report with:

[list]
[*][b]FUSE.log[/b] (written next to Railroader's Player.log)
[*][b]Player.log[/b]
[*]The output of [b]/fuse.report[/b]
[*]The output of [b]/fuse.loaded[/b] if package state matters, and [b]/fuse.conflicts[/b] if conflicts are reported
[/list]

[line]

[size=4][b]Source and license[/b][/size]

FUSE is free software, released under the [b]GNU Affero General Public License v3.0[/b]. Source code, the converter, the bulk installer, and the complete toolchain bundle are all on GitHub.
